Multics port of Dave Platt's 550-point Adventure, which was written
for CP-6 while Dave Platt worked for Honeywell's Los Angeles
Development Center (LADC), formerly the Xerox CP-V development
team (see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honeywell_CP-6 and
https://www.computerhistory.org/collections/catalog/102733957/ for
background).

This port is modified from Mike Arnautov's port, 10-11 September 2026,
by Jim Lippard with assistance from Claude Opus 4.8.

This directory contains Unix unified diffs from the files in

https://github.com/Quuxplusone/Advent/tree/master/PLAT0550

which will make it compile and run on Multics. The files required
from that distribution are ADVENTURE.ACODE, munge.f, munge.i,
exec.f, and exec.i.  The munge.i include file requires no
changes.

The Multics versions can be created on a Unix system using
  patch oldfile <newfile.diff
or by using the unix_patch.pl1 on Multics as:
   unix_patch oldfile newfile.diff -of newfile

The main issues that had to be addressed were:
(1) Multics Fortran 77 very strictly enforces ANSI standards
on string bounds and arrays (and lacks INCLUDE, see below).
(2) Multics has 36-bit words, not 32-bit, so some calculations
had to be modified.
(3) Multics has a persistent process, requiring manual resets
in the munge database creator to avoid monotonic increase in
database size on multiple runs in the same process; similarly
we close the database files before open, ignoring errors, on
each run of the game in case the player has broken out of
a game. (Alternative manual fix is a new_proc; attempting to
io_call close/detach file09 confuses fortran_io_.)

This version will compile without errors and fixes one
latent bug in exec.f from the version above: CHRPOS
at statement number 9998 in the subroutine SAY should
be CHARPOS. (This has no impact on the game and appears
to be dead code, but it's necessary on Multics if you
want a warning-free compilation.)

If imported to Multics via method that wraps >80 char lines,
main impact is ADVENTURE.ACODE, which will wrap these:

2074: (102)          [A/Several] ball[/s] of searing flame [leaps/burst] out of your magic ring, [bounces/rebound]
2075: (96)          off of the ground, and [melts/vaporize] the kni[fe/ves] before [it/they] can reach you!
2544: (81)          gets cut to shreds by [his/their] nasty sharp kni[fe/ves]!  The tattered

My ADVENTURE.ACODE also came in via copy_cards with an octal 032
(CTL-Z) at end of file which should be removed.

There shouldn't be any line length issues with the fortran code.

Multics Fortran 77 (new_fortran) does not support INCLUDE files, so use
expand_fortran as follows:

   expand_fortran munge.f munge.fortran
   expand_fortran exec.f exec.fortran

This will include (many times!) the munge.i and exec.i include files
in place, with comments around it to show you where they went.
Note that expand_fortran currently does not check for the existence
of the output file and will happily overwrite anything.

Then compile (new_fortran wants a .fortran suffix):

   new_fortran munge.fortran -ansi77 -table -card
   new_fortran exec.fortran -ansi77 -table -card

Build the game databases:

   munge ADVENTURE.ACODE

This port expects the resulting databases (ADVINITn.DAT, where n is 1 through 4)
to be in >site>adv550_dir; if you want them somewhere else you only need to change
the four filenames specified in exec.fortran.

Finally, rename the exec binary to adv550:

   rn exec adv550

and put it wherever you want the executable to live.


NOTE:  munge.f contains this code in FUNCTION MASH that is not
ideal:

      IF (J4 .GT. 2147483647 .OR. J4 .LT. -2147483648) THEN
          J4 = MOD(J4, 2147483647)
      ENDIF      

Alternatives to try are:

C     Replicate 32-bit signed wraparound to avoid 36-bit overflow trap,
C     matching the original algorithm's overflow behavior.
      IF (J4 .GE. 2147483648) J4 = J4 - 4294967296
      IF (J4 .LT. -2147483648) J4 = J4 + 4294967296

and:

20    MASH = MOD(J4 / 65536, 65536)
      IF (MASH.LT.0) MASH=-MASH

But it works as is, and I plan to try out these other options
later. On OpenBSD, munge produces a vocabulary size of 435 and
on Multics it produces a vocabulary size of 436 and I think this
might be the root of it.
